{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkzxNPpqxWCMGPEjsXd8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written all three files for candidate 1, and the admission self-check prints `OK`.\n\n**Model name:** `most_surprising_chain_maximum`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People judge a sequence by its single most striking stretch. That is either its longest unbroken chain of repeats (a streak) or its longest unbroken chain of switches (strict alternation), whichever is less likely in a sequence of that length from their picture of a fair coin. That coin has a fitted believed switch rate, which comes out at about 0.72. Only that one most unlikely stretch counts. The sequence whose worst stretch is less unlikely is chosen, and people differ in how decisive they are and in which side they lean towards.\n\n**Why this one:**\n- **It fits the brief:** the brief asked for a maximum over parts of the sequence rather than a sum, and the model takes the larger of two surprises.\n- **It targets the critique's one finding that survives FDR (q = 0.048):** in long pairs where both sequences have at most two switches, people pick the more-switching sequence more often than the current best model predicts. The first one or two breaks in a streak shorten its longest run sharply, so this mechanism rewards them strongly.\n- **It differs from earlier maximum-type models:** `most_lopsided_window_alarm`, `longest_patterned_stretch`, `worst_stretch_regularity_evidence` and `streak_tolerance_alarm` used heads/tails imbalance in a window, motif copy counts, Bayesian evidence over sub-stretches, or penalties summed over runs. This one uses the exact probability, under the believed-switch-rate coin, that the longest chain is at least as long as observed.\n\n**Speed:** My first version passed 33 coefficient columns per trial, and its smoke fit ran for over 16 minutes. I rewrote it so the 64 possible (length, chain length) cases are computed once in the graph and each trial just looks its case up. That gives the same log-probability at the starting point, but each gradient takes 1.9 ms instead of 8 ms.\n\n**Fit:**\n- **Smoke check:** loads, finite log-probability, data contract holds, finite ELPD-LOO of −4333 on the smoke fit.\n- **My own longer fit (1,000 tune + 300 draws, one chain):** no divergences, tree depth settled at 4.\n- **Time:** that rate projects to about 9 minutes per chain for production's 3,000 tune + 4,000 draws, assuming the four chains run in parallel.\n\n**Not checked:** novelty against the live models, and convergence across several chains (R-hat), which a single chain can't report.\n\n**Expected outcome:** the smoke-fit ELPD is far behind the best model's −3567. Earlier standalone maximum-type models lost by 100 to 750 nats, so this will probably lose by a wide margin too."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1344,"cache_read_input_tokens":107586,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1344,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b8ba64f-0d44-49e7-a5b2-64d88975a409","uuid":"9ea6bf83-0239-497d-a662-15e41110e92e","timestamp":"2026-10-06T12:41:09.147Z","request_id":"req_011CfkzxMR3X4Rf7HTxDQmCo"}
